(1832217, 246715, 36231, '<td valign="top">OK ... so you have 26 comments that tell you the photo is out of focus. Here''s what you can do to stop this from happening again:<br/> <br/>Most "point and shoot" cameras, and I''m sure this includes yours, has an autofocus feature with an assist.  When you go to take a photo, press lightly on your shutter button, and let the camera focus itself. It should signify it''s in focus with a beep, or a red square of somesort. Mine beeps and shows a red square right where it''s focused.  If you don''t get that, it''s not focusing.  Low light always makes it more difficult. One it is in focus, press the button the rest of the way down.  Take several photos at different angles to help you get the best perspective.  Also, try a different background next time.  Quilts can be a little busy.<br/> <br/>Don''t give up!  Check out my stuff, and see that I still haven''t! :) Try again in one of the new challenges.<br/> <br/>Good luck! :)<br/>Tara</td>', 'OK ... so you have 26 comments that tell you the photo is out of focus. Here''s what you can do to stop this from happening again: Most "point and shoot" cameras, and I''m sure this includes yours, has an autofocus feature with an assist.  When you go to take a photo, press lightly on your shutter button, and let the camera focus itself. It should signify it''s in focus with a beep, or a red square of somesort. Mine beeps and shows a red square right where it''s focused.  If you don''t get that, it''s not focusing.  Low light always makes it more difficult. One it is in focus, press the button the rest of the way down.  Take several photos at different angles to help you get the best perspective.  Also, try a different background next time.  Quilts can be a little busy. Don''t give up!  Check out my stuff, and see that I still haven''t! :) Try again in one of the new challenges. Good luck! :)Tara', '2005-11-02 07:14:25.000000', NULL, 0),
